G12.b:
⟪ἄ-βυσσος⟫, -ον
(< Ion. βυσσός = βυθός),
⟪1.⟫ in cl., ⟪boundless, bottomless⟫ (e.g. ἄ. πέλαγος, ἄ. πλοῦτος Æsch.).
⟪2.⟫ [In LXX (for ⁨תְּהוֹם⁩, ⟦Genesis 1:2|Ge 1:2⟧, al.; exc. ⟦Isaiah 44:27|Is 44:27⟧, ⟦Job 41:22|Jb 41:22⟧ for ⁨צוּלָה⁩, ⁨מְצוּלָה⁩, ⟦Job 36:16|Jb 36:16⟧ for ⁨רַחַב⁩) and] NT, as subst. (MM, VGT, s.v.), ἡ ἄ. (sc. χώρα), ⟪the abyss⟫;
	⟪(a)⟫ of the sea (⟦Genesis 1:2|Ge 1:2⟧);
	⟪(b)⟫ of the underworld, as the abode of the dead: ⟦Romans 10:7|Ro 10:7⟧ (a paraphrase of ⟦Deuteronomy 30:13|De 30:13⟧ LXX); as the abode of demons, ⟦Luke 8:31|Lk 8:31⟧, ⟦Revelation 9:1|Re 9:1⟧, ⟦Revelation 9:2|2⟧, ⟦Revelation 9:11|11⟧, ⟦Revelation 11:7|11:7⟧, ⟦Revelation 17:8|17:8⟧, ⟦Revelation 20:1|20:1⟧, ⟦Revelation 20:3|3⟧ (Cremer, 2).†

G15.b:
⟪ἀγαθο-ποιέω⟫, -ῶ (= cl. ἀγαθὸν ποιεῖν, εὐεργετεῖν),
[in LXX: ⟦Numbers 10:32|Nu 10:32⟧, ⟦Judges 17:13|Jg 17:13⟧ A, ⟦Zephaniah 1:12|Ze 1:12⟧ (⁨יטב⁩ hi.), To 12:13 B, I Mac 11:33, II Mac 1:2*;] ⟪to do good⟫;
⟪(a)⟫ univ.: ⟦1 Peter 2:15|I Pe 2:15⟧, ⟦1 Peter 2:20|20⟧, ⟦1 Peter 3:6|3:6⟧, ⟦1 Peter 3:17|17⟧, ⟦3 John 1:11|III Jo 11⟧;
⟪(b)⟫ for another's benefit: ⟦Mark 3:4|Mk 3:4⟧ (T, ἀγαθὸν ποιῆσαι), ⟦Luke 6:9|Lk 6:9⟧;
⟪(c)⟫ acc. pers., ⟦Luke 6:33|Lk 6:33⟧, ⟦Luke 6:35|35⟧ (Cremer, 8).†

G26.b:
† ⟪ἀγάπη⟫, -ης, ἡ
[in LXX for ⁨אַהֲבָה⁩, which is also rendered by ἀγάπησις and φιλία;] ⟪love⟫, ⟪goodwill⟫, ⟪esteem⟫. Outside of bibl. and eccl. books, there is no clear instance (with Deiss., LAE, 18:4, 70:2, cf. the same writer in Constr. Quar., ii, 4; and with MM, VGT, s.v., cf. Dr. Moulton in Exp. Times, xxvi, 3, 139). In NT, like ἀγαπάω, -ῶ,
⟪1.⟫ Of men's love:
	⟪(a)⟫ to one another, ⟦John 13:35|Jo 13:35⟧;
	⟪(b)⟫ to God, ⟦1 John 2:5|I Jo 2:5⟧.
⟪2.⟫ Of divine love;
	⟪(a)⟫ God's love: to men, ⟦Romans 5:8|Ro 5:8⟧; to Christ, ⟦John 17:26|Jo 17:26⟧;
	⟪(b)⟫ Christ's love to men: ⟦Romans 8:35|Ro 8:35⟧.
⟪3.⟫ In pl., ⟪love feasts⟫: ⟦Jude 1:12|Ju 12⟧ (DB, iii, 157).
SYN.: φιλία. ἀ., signifying properly (v.s. ἀγαπάω) love which chooses its object, is taken over from LXX, where its connotation is more general, into NT, and there used exclusively to express that spiritual bond of love between God and man and between man and man, in Christ, which is characteristic of Christianity. It is thus distinct from φιλία, friendship (⟦James 4:4|Ja 4:4⟧ only), στοργή, natural affection (in NT only in compounds, v.s. ἄστοργος) and ἔρως, sexual love, which is not used in NT, its place being taken by ἐπιθυμία. (Cf. ἀγαπάω; and v. Abbott, Essays, 70f.; DB, vol. i., 555; Cremer, 13, 593; MM, VGT, s.v.).

G27.b:
⟪ἀγαπητός⟫, -ή, -όν
(< ἀγαπάω), [in LXX chiefly for ⁨יָתִיד⁩, ⁨יָדִיד⁩;] ⟪beloved⟫ (v. M, Pr., 221);
⟪(a)⟫ by God: of Christ, ⟦Matthew 3:17|Mt 3:17⟧; of men, ⟦Romans 1:7|Ro 1:7⟧;
⟪(b)⟫ by Christians, of one another: ⟦1 Corinthians 4:14|I Co 4:14⟧; freq. as form of address, ⟦1 Corinthians 10:14|I Co 10:14⟧; opp. to ἐχθρός, ⟦Romans 11:28|Ro 11:28⟧ (v. AR, Eph., 229; Cremer, 17; MM, VGT, s.v.).

G48.b:
⟪ἁγνίζω⟫ (> ἁγνός),
[in LXX always ceremonially, chiefly for ⁨קדשׁ⁩;] ⟪to purify⟫, ⟪cleanse from defilement⟫;
⟪(a)⟫ ceremonially: ⟦John 11:55|Jo 11:55⟧, ⟦Acts 21:24|Ac 21:24⟧, ⟦Acts 21:26|26⟧ ⟦Acts 24:18|24:18⟧;
⟪(b)⟫ morally: ⟦James 4:8|Ja 4:8⟧, ⟦1 Peter 1:22|I Pe 1:22⟧, ⟦1 John 3:3|I Jo 3:3⟧.†
SYN.: καθαρίζω, q.v. (and v.s. ἁγνός).
